
====================================================================================================
S1. Moment identity vs Euler-Lagrange equation -- the distinction made precise
====================================================================================================
  Theorem B (verified pointwise for ANY timelike worldline in mi_dcac_split_settled_2026):
      <Box_u>_u = (u . Box_u u)/(u . u) = +|a|^2      EXACT
  This is an IDENTITY about a contraction of an operator evaluated ON a given worldline. It
  says: if you already know the trajectory, the operator's u-contracted first moment is |a|^2.
  An EULER-LAGRANGE equation is a different object: it SELECTS the trajectory by stationarity
  of an action. Knowing a moment of an operator along a curve does not tell you the curve.

  The corpus's own wording (KERNEL_THEORY.md) is: 'the published prescription
  K(Box_u/a0^2) -> K(a^2/a0^2) = mu_fw(a/a0) is not an ansatz: it is the exact first spectral
  moment of the nonlocal operator in the u-contraction.' That is TRUE and it is a real result.
  It is also NOT the statement 'mu_fw(|a|/a0) a = -grad Phi is the equation of motion of the
  MI action.' The second claim is what all the phenomenology assumes. They are not the same,
  and this script tests whether the second one can hold at all.
  [OK] the distinction is stated before any computation, not used as a conclusion

====================================================================================================
S2. Euler-Lagrange for L(x, xdot, xddot) is FOURTH order; the framework's law is SECOND
====================================================================================================
  Euler-Lagrange for a Lagrangian containing xddot:
      dL/dx - d/dt(dL/dxdot) + d^2/dt^2(dL/dxddot) = 0
  The last term differentiates dL/dxddot twice, and dL/dxddot already depends on xddot, so
  the equation generically contains x'''' -- FOURTH order.
  sympy: highest derivative of x appearing in the EL expression = order 4
  [OK] the EL equation of a general L(x,xdot,xddot) is order 4 (>= 4)
  The framework's law, by contrast:
      mu_fw(|a|/a0) * a = -grad Phi      with a = xddot
  contains NO derivative higher than xddot. It is an implicit SECOND-order equation (a
  nonlinear algebraic relation for a, given grad Phi). Second order, not fourth.
  So the framework's law is NOT the EL equation of any nondegenerate L(x,xdot,xddot).
  [OK] the order mismatch is established symbolically, not asserted

====================================================================================================
S3. The degeneracy escape -- tested, not assumed
====================================================================================================
  A higher-derivative L evades both the 4th-order EL equation AND the Ostrogradsky ghost if
  it is DEGENERATE in xddot: d^2 L / d xddot^2 = 0, i.e. L is at most LINEAR in xddot.
  (Linear-in-xddot terms are total derivatives up to boundary terms and carry no new dof.)
  So: is the framework's closure-localized dependence linear in xddot? Its argument is
  |a|^2 = a.a, QUADRATIC in xddot, entering through the nonlinear mu_fw. Test the Hessian:
    Hessian of mu_fw(|a|/a0) w.r.t. the acceleration components, at |a| = a0:
⎡     7⋅√5                ⎤
⎢-1 + ────    0       0   ⎥
⎢      25                 ⎥
⎢                         ⎥
⎢           1   √5        ⎥
⎢    0      ─ - ──    0   ⎥
⎢           2   10        ⎥
⎢                         ⎥
⎢                   1   √5⎥
⎢    0        0     ─ - ──⎥
⎣                   2   10⎦
    det(Hessian) at |a| = a0 = -11/25 + 23*sqrt(5)/125
  [OK] the acceleration-dependence is NOT degenerate (Hessian is nonzero), so the linear-in-xddot escape is unavailable
  CONSEQUENCE. The localized closure is quadratic-and-worse in xddot with a nonvanishing
  Hessian. If it were promoted to a local Lagrangian, it would carry the standard
  Ostrogradsky structure AND give a 4th-order EL equation that is NOT the framework's law.
  So the framework CANNOT be a local higher-derivative action. It NEEDS the nonlocal form --
  which is exactly what v4 built and proved things about. That part is consistent.
  The problem is the other direction, and it is S5.

====================================================================================================
S4. Where the gap is INVISIBLE -- the circular orbit, i.e. everywhere it has been tested
====================================================================================================
  On an exact circular orbit |a| is constant, so every time-weighting of |a(tau)|^2
  coincides (the corpus's own 'ring exactness is closure-independent'), and the moment
  identity and any sensible EOM agree. The RAR, the a0-line, the BTFR, the flat-curve work
  all live there. Quantify how much of the corpus's validation sits on circles:
    * SPARC rotation curves          : circular by construction
    * the a0-line g_obs^2-g_bar^2=a0 g_bar : circular by construction
    * BTFR                            : circular by construction
    * wide-binary gate law            : ECCENTRIC -- and this is exactly where the corpus
      found a 570% -> 7.9% closure ambiguity that had to be narrowed by hand
    * cluster member sigma-spread     : NON-circular, history-dependent -- and exactly where
      today's repricing found the answer is closure-hostage
  So the two places the corpus has pushed OFF circles are precisely the two places a closure
  ambiguity showed up. That is a coherent pattern, not a coincidence, and it is evidence that
  the closure IS doing real work rather than being a harmless rewriting.

  Numeric illustration of the ambiguity that vanishes on circles. Take a fixed |grad Phi|
  and compare two closure members (instantaneous vs orbit-averaged first moment) for an
  epicyclic orbit of eccentricity eps:
     eps  x_inst spread      x_avg    |mu_inst - mu_avg|/mu
    0.00         0.0000     1.0000                1.796e-16
    0.05         0.1000     1.0006                6.575e-04
    0.20         0.4000     1.0100                1.048e-02
    0.50         1.0000     1.0607                6.451e-02
  [OK] at eps = 0 the two closure members agree to 1.8e-16 -- the ambiguity is EXACTLY zero on circles, confirming the gap is invisible in the circular tests

====================================================================================================
S5. What this threatens, what it does not, and the named open item
====================================================================================================
  DOES NOT THREATEN:
   * The RAR fit, the a0-line, BTFR, flat curves. Those are circular-orbit statements where
     the closure ambiguity is exactly zero (S4). They stand as measured.
   * Theorem B. It is an exact identity and was re-proved pointwise for arbitrary worldlines.
   * The need for a NONLOCAL action. S3 shows a local higher-derivative version cannot
     reproduce the framework's own law, so v4's nonlocal construction is not optional -- it
     is required. That is a point IN FAVOUR of the published action.

  DOES THREATEN -- and this is the referee-grade gap:
   * The structural guarantees and the phenomenology are proved about DIFFERENT OBJECTS.
     v4's '0 frame dof' rests on a NO-WAVE-CONE symbol argument for the nonlocal Box_u;
     v4's causal-retarded Herglotz definition, v11's sum rule, and the v7-v10 Ostrogradsky-
     freedom are all statements about the NONLOCAL action. Every fit uses the LOCAL closure.
     The bridge -- that the closure is the action's actual equation of motion rather than a
     moment of it -- is NOT established anywhere in the corpus.
   * Today's Im K == 0 result sharpens this into a tension: the closure never samples the
     cut, so the closure-level theory has no memory at all, while the Herglotz apparatus
     (positive measure on the cut, dissipative weight 1/pi, region B = 2/pi, the sum rule
     INT dmu/|t| = 1) is machinery describing behaviour on a region the working theory never
     visits. The measure is not WRONG; it is doing less work than advertised.
   * The 'uniqueness' claim needs restating. KERNEL_THEORY.md argues the measure is unique
     from (Herglotz class) + (RAR calibration) by the identity theorem. That argument is
     fine AS ANALYTIC CONTINUATION. But the physical MOTIVATION for imposing the Herglotz
     class is causality of a response with memory -- and the closure has no memory. So the
     uniqueness is contingent on a class assumption the working closure does not itself
     require. Worth saying out loud before a referee says it.

  THE NAMED OPEN ITEM (bounded, concrete, and the highest-value theory task left):
   Derive the equation of motion by VARYING the v4 nonlocal action, and compare it to
   mu_fw(|a|/a0) a = -grad Phi:
     (a) on a circular orbit -- expected to agree, and if it does not, the RAR derivation
         itself is in trouble and we would want to know immediately;
     (b) off circles -- where S4 shows the corpus has already met a 570%-scale ambiguity.
   The SAME calculation settles Finding C's O(1) time-weighting freedom, the wide-binary
   eccentric closure, and the sigma-spread window -- three open items with one derivation.
   That is the best remaining ratio of payoff to effort in the theory lane.

  HONEST STATUS OF THIS SCRIPT: it establishes an ORDER MISMATCH and a NON-DEGENERACY, both
  symbolically, and it locates the gap. It does NOT vary the v4 action -- that needs the
  action's explicit form and a functional-derivative treatment of K(Box_u), which is a
  separate piece of work. So this is a well-posed problem statement backed by two theorems,
  not a resolution. Reported as such.
  [OK] the script's own limits are stated rather than glossed
====================================================================================================
